8.text:
The coat is not a described object in the sense of colour, cut or wear. It is a functional leit-motif that Chekhov brings in only at the points at which Ochumelov changes colour.

In the 1884 Russian text and in the Garnett / standard English circulation that is conventionally read as three symbolic descriptive beats, i.e. the three classic on-off-on shifts of the chameleon mentality:

* Arrival / self-importance: Ochumelov comes out “in his overcoat and cap”, in Russian в шинели и фуражке, the new sheepskin / overcoat being the outward sign of official self-importance;
* Recoil / deference: the garment is noticed against the first change of attitude to the dog, the coat being tightened/buttoned / collar up as the dog is re-identified as General Zhigalov’s;
* Re-assertion / retreat: the coat is loosened / unbuttoned when the dog is re-classified as a stray/cur and authority is re-asserted, and it is re-buttoned / put straight again on the final departure.

That triadic buttoned → loosened → re-buttoned movement is the three descriptive/symbolic uses on which the critical commonplace is built. In a literal word count the garment is verbally brought in four times in the Russian original and in the Garnett-type English - the entrance reference, the first tightening, the loosening/unbuttoning and the final re-buttoning on departure - which is normally collapsed into the three substantive symbolic shifts quoted above.

7.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE:

The coat is not a described object in the ordinary pictorial sense in Chekhov. The Russian шинель / пальто and the Garnett overcoat/sheepskin are not given colour, cut or wear. What Chekhov gives is a functional stage business with the garment that the criticism has read as a symbolic barometer of Ochumelov/Otchumyelov’s chameleon-like fear.

In the 1884 Russian text and in the Garnett/standard English circulation that business is brought in at each change of attitude and is conventionally collapsed into three symbolic re-descriptions, four overt verbal mentions:

* Entrance / establishing: Ochumelov comes out “in a new sheepskin coat / in his overcoat and cap”, the sign of official self-importance;
* First recoil / deference: the coat is buttoned up / drawn tight / handled as he shifts to obsequious fear at the thought of the General’s dog;
* Re-assertion / aggressive volte-face: the coat is loosened / unbuttoned as he recovers a bluster against a ‘stray’;
* Exit: the coat is put straight / buttoned up again on departure.

The three substantive shifts — proud → fearful/deferential → aggressive/re-assertive — are the triadic chameleon pattern that commentary quotes. Literally counted in the English text that is four mentions of the garment, three symbolic descriptive beats.

If “describe” is taken in the strict literary sense of a visual descriptive portrait, Chekhov describes the coat zero times; the familiar “symbolic descriptions” are a critical schematisation of those three/four functional mentions.

